/*
===============================================================================
DISPATCHERS.DEFAULT vs DISPATCHERS.IO
===============================================================================

INTERNAL IMPLEMENTATION (IMPORTANT):

    Dispatchers.Default  ┐
                         ├── SAME underlying thread pool
    Dispatchers.IO       ┘

✔ Both Dispatchers.Default and Dispatchers.IO execute on the SAME underlying
  thread pool internally.
✔ This shared pool is implemented as the "Default Dispatcher".

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

WHY THREAD NAMES LOOK THE SAME:

When you log thread names, you may see:

    DefaultDispatcher-worker-1
    DefaultDispatcher-worker-3
    DefaultDispatcher-worker-7
    ...

This happens EVEN WHEN using Dispatchers.IO.

❌ There is NO separate thread naming like:
    "IODispatcher-worker-1"

Thread names come from the SHARED underlying thread pool,
not from the dispatcher itself.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

WHAT ACTUALLY DIFFERS BETWEEN DEFAULT AND IO:

Dispatcher ≠ Thread Pool

Dispatcher controls:
✔ Task scheduling behavior
✔ Allowed parallelism
✔ Type of workload optimization

Thread pool controls:
✔ The actual threads on which code executes

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DISPATCHERS.DEFAULT
- Designed for CPU-bound work
- Uses limited parallelism
- Parallelism roughly equals number of CPU cores
- Suitable for:
    * Computation
    * Data processing
    * In-memory transformations

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DISPATCHERS.IO
- Designed for blocking I/O operations
- Allows significantly higher parallelism
- Can scale up to a large number of threads (≈ 64)
- Reuses threads from the Default dispatcher pool

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

KEY POINT TO REMEMBER:

Dispatchers.IO is NOT a separate thread pool.
It is a different scheduling strategy built on top of
the SAME thread pool used by Dispatchers.Default.

===============================================================================
*/
